A||N||4||Formula88||Tips on installing V6 Alternator?||||5||
Z||000000||Formula88||11-13-2001||02:32 PM||||I read the typical search results.  Getting to the alternator and the old one out was a piece of cake.  Thanks for the info, everyone.  My question comes in installing the new one.  It seems terribly difficult to get the top bolt in the alternator bracket.  Actually, I can't get the alternator in the bracket enough to try even to put the bolt in.  I've pushed and pry'ed and anything else you can image.  I ended up breaking the stupid bracket trying to get it in.  Well, I've found a replacement, but I don't want a repeat performance....  sooo,<BR>any suggestions?  I'm thinking about mounting the bracket on the alternator out of the car and installing it as an assembly.  Also, any other caveats about the install?<P>It really shouldn't be this difficult.  Took me all of 30 minutes to get the alt. out.  I figured it's a piece of cake swap.  Should have been able to do it in about an hour if I could have gotten that upper bolt in. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||rogergarrison||11-13-2001||02:58 PM|||| My V6 is ez. I put the big top pivot bolt in first so I can jiggle it around, then put the belt tension one in. If the big bolts got any corrosion on it, sand it lightly and put some oil or silicone spray on it. My first one was so corroded i had to use a bearing press to get it out.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 11-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Formula88||11-13-2001||03:04 PM||||Well, the problem wasn't getting the bolt into the bolt hole.  The problem is getting the alternator to fit in the bracket at all.  It seemed to be an insanely tight fit.  I'm thinking the sides of the bolt boss on the alternator should barely tough the bracket at all, but mine was wedged so tight I couldn't get it in.  I also had to yank on it pretty good to get it out after removing the bolt in the first place.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Rodrv6||11-13-2001||04:05 PM||||There is a steel sleeve pressed into one of the holes in the aluminum bracket.  When the pivot bolt is tightened, the sleeve is pushed against the alternator to take up any space.  You may be able to carefully push the bushing back into the hole a little to give you some clearance.  I've done it using a socket and a bench vise.  Doesn't take much movement.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula88||11-13-2001||09:31 PM||||I'll bet that's it.  I noticed the sleeve, but it looked like it wouldn't budge.  I'll "gently" tap it some for clearance.  <BR>||||reg||1
